Fastify registers **two** content-type parsers out of the box:

| Content-Type | Default charset | Parser |
|---|---|---|
| `application/json` | `utf-8` | `secureJsonParse` (proto/constructor poisoning protection) |
| `text/plain` | `utf-8` | pass-through (returns raw body string as-is) |

**Source evidence:**

- **`types/content-type-parser.d.ts`, line 38** (JSDoc on `AddContentTypeParser`):
  > "Natively, Fastify only supports `'application/json'` and `'text/plain'` content types. The default charset is utf-8."

- **`lib/content-type-parser.js`, lines 306–325** — the two implementations:
  ```js
  // application/json
  function getDefaultJsonParser(onProtoPoisoning, onConstructorPoisoning) {
    return function defaultJsonParser(req, body, done) {
      if (body.length === 0) { done(new FST_ERR_CTP_EMPTY_JSON_BODY()); return }
      try { done(null, secureJsonParse(body, parseOptions)) }
      catch { done(new FST_ERR_CTP_INVALID_JSON_BODY()) }
    }
  }

  // text/plain
  function defaultPlainTextParser(req, body, done) {
    done(null, body)  // raw pass-through
  }
  ```

**Key behaviours:**
- Both can be **overridden** via `fastify.addContentTypeParser('application/json', ...)` / `addContentTypeParser('text/plain', ...)`.
- The JSON parser has configurable `protoAction` / `constructorAction` to guard against prototype poisoning.
- Any other content-type (e.g. `multipart/form-data`, `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`) has **no built-in parser** — you must register one yourself or use a plugin.

`defaultSchemaErrorFormatter` is defined in **`lib/context.js` at line 87**:

```js
function defaultSchemaErrorFormatter (errors, dataVar) {
  let text = ''
  const separator = ', '

  for (let i = 0; i !== errors.length; ++i) {
    const e = errors[i]
    text += dataVar + (e.instancePath || '') + ' ' + e.message + separator
  }
  return new Error(text.slice(0, -separator.length))
}
```

It iterates over an array of AJV validation errors, concatenates each one as `<dataVar><instancePath> <message>`, and returns a single `Error` with the joined string (trailing separator stripped).
